<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diego Columbus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1479–<a href="/years/1526">1526</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Diego Colón</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diego Columbus was the eldest son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> and governor of the Indies at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> from 1509. Pursuing his father's hereditary claims against the Spanish crown, he won recognition as admiral and viceroy, sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-esquivel">Juan de Esquivel</a> to settle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> in 1509 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-velazquez">Diego Velázquez</a> to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> in 1511. He was recalled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1523 and died in 1526.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diego-columbus.jpg" alt="Diego Columbus" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diego_Colon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Diego Columbus was born in Porto Santo, Portugal, in 1479 or 1480, the child of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> and Filipa Moniz Perestrelo. He became a page at the Spanish court in 1492, the year of his father's first voyage. With his half-brother Fernando he was a page to Prince Don Juan, and in 1497 to Queen Isabella.</p><p>He spent most of his adult life trying to regain the titles and privileges granted to his father and denied in 1500. His marriage to María de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a> y Rojas, a niece of the 2nd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fernando-alvarez-de-toledo-3rd-duke-of-alba">Duke of Alba</a> and a cousin of King Ferdinand, greatly helped his cause. Ferdinand had forced the match.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In August 1508 Diego was named Governor of the Indies, the post his father had held, and he reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> in July 1509. He made his home there in the Alcázar de Colón, which still stands. In 1511 a royal council declared <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> to be under his power by right of his father, confirmed his titles of viceroy and admiral as hereditary but honorific, and gave him a tenth of the net royal income. Uraba and Veragua were excluded. Factions soon formed between his followers and Ferdinand's royal officials, and in 1514 the king recalled him. He spent five years in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> pressing his claims, and in 1520 Charles restored his powers.</p><p>He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a> on 12 November 1520, during a native revolt near the Franciscan missions on the Cumana River. In 1521 he invested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a>'s plan to settle the Cumana area. Its failure was blamed on Diego and cost him the king's confidence. On 26 December 1522 enslaved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jolof-empire">Jolof</a> workers on his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> plantation began the first major slave rebellion in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>. Many escaped into the mountains and founded maroon communities among the surviving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a>; many others were captured, and Diego had them hanged.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Charles reprimanded Diego in 1523 for his defiance of royal power on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and for the Cumana failure, and recalled him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. He died there on 23 February 1526, and his rents, offices and titles in the New World fell into dispute among his descendants. In 1536 a compromise made his son Luis Colón de Toledo Admiral of the Indies. Luis gave up all other rights in return for an annuity of 10,000 ducats, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a> as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fief</a>, an estate on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-panama">Isthmus of Panama</a> and the titles of Duke of Veragua and Marquess of Jamaica.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Diego Columbus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1509 Juan de Esquivel, sent by <b>Diego Colón</b>, founded Sevilla la Nueva on the north coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-jamaica">Spanish Jamaica</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in Montalbán, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.83,-0.8&amp;year=1509" aria-label="Montalbán on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Diego Columbus"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4948 4079h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4948 4079h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=40.83,-0.8&amp;year=1509">What was here in 1509? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
